#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook. Injects a static pointer to the router and nothing else.
#
# STATIC IS THE POINT. Prompt caching holds only while the front of the request is byte-identical,
# so this must print the same bytes on every run, forever. No date, no branch, no git status, no
# counter, no reading of any file that changes. One volatile byte here costs the cache on every
# request in every session.
#
# THE ONE EXCEPTION, AND ITS BOUND. It reads `conventions.response_style` from the project profile.
# That is a file that changes, which the rule above forbids, so the bound matters: the output has
# exactly two forms, and it moves between them only when a person deliberately edits the key. That
# costs one cache miss on the session after the edit, the same as editing CLAUDE.md, rather than one
# per request. A key read for its value would be volatile; a key read for which of two paragraphs to
# print is not.
#
# NO python3 HERE. This runs before every session, so it must not depend on an interpreter being
# present or pay its startup. The profile is matched with a builtin `case`, which also means the
# match is textual: `"response_style": "verbose"` and `"response_style":"verbose"` are both
# recognised and an exotic formatting of the same key is not. A missed match yields terse, which is
# the default, so the failure mode is the documented behaviour rather than a broken session.
#
# Budget: under 250 tokens, 400 hard, enforced by tests/validate-skills.sh.

set -euo pipefail

read -r -d '' CONTEXT <<'TXT' || true
This project uses keel. Before starting substantive work, pick a skill.

Discover and define: repo-snapshot, shape-idea, write-prd, write-user-stories, design-architecture.
Build: write-plan, execute-plan, tdd, debug, coding-standards.
Verify: review-code, security-audit, refactor, optimize-performance.
Ship: setup-deployment, ship, write-docs. Production is down now: incident-response, before debug.
Porting: port-assess, or apex-export then apex-port-plan for Oracle APEX.
Meta: create-skill, context-budget. Unsure which fits: use the keel skill to route.

Rules: process skills come first and may invoke others (debug before tdd for a bug, shape-idea
before write-prd for a rough idea). Announce the skill in one line, then follow it.
Answer directly when no skill fits; forcing one is worse.

Project facts, verify commands, and gates are in .keel/profile.json. Read it before running any
test, lint, or build command rather than assuming the stack's usual one.
TXT

read -r -d '' BREVITY <<'TXT' || true

Replies stay brief; artifacts stay full. Say what changed, where it is, and what needs a decision.
Never omit which checks ran or were skipped, assumptions, deviations, or a Done when command's output.
TXT

# Walk up for the profile, the way sensitive-guard does. A session started in a subdirectory is
# ordinary, and looking only in the current directory silently loses the project's choice.
profile=""
d="$PWD"
while [ -n "$d" ]; do
    if [ -f "$d/.keel/profile.json" ]; then profile="$d/.keel/profile.json"; break; fi
    [ "$d" = "/" ] && break
    d="$(dirname "$d")"
done

# Terse unless the project asked for verbose. Absent profile, absent key and unreadable file all
# mean terse, because that is the default and a hook is not the place to report a bad profile.
#
# The `-r` test is load bearing and was added after review caught it. This script runs under
# `set -e`, and the assignment is the last command in the chain, so a read that fails takes the
# whole hook down: exit 1, nothing on stdout, and the session loses the router pointer entirely
# rather than just the brevity rule. A 000-mode profile is rare; a session that silently looks
# like keel is not installed is expensive.
style_text=""
[ -n "$profile" ] && [ -r "$profile" ] && style_text="$(<"$profile")"
case "$style_text" in
    *'"response_style": "verbose"'*|*'"response_style":"verbose"'*) ;;
    *) CONTEXT="$CONTEXT$BREVITY" ;;
esac

escape() { local s="$1"; s="${s//\\/\\\\}"; s="${s//\"/\\\"}"; s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"; printf '%s' "$s"; }

printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$(escape "$CONTEXT")"
